/** @page odbcsandboxguide Connections from Sandboxed Code

    @tableofcontents

    @section odbc_sandbox_intro Introduction

    When the connection values of a \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool were chosen by code under a sandbox that
    restricts network access, %Qore checks the destination of each connection against the network policy of that
    sandbox before the driver connects.  Where an ODBC connection goes depends on its ODBC driver, so the module
    determines the destination only for the drivers below, from the keys of the connection string merged with those
    of its DSN, which are read through the driver manager (\c SQLGetPrivateProfileString(): \c odbc.ini and
    \c odbcinst.ini, the user DSN before the system one, the connection string before the DSN), so that the
    resolution is the driver manager's.  A connection that the policy allows connects only to the checked address,
    also when it is made again after it was lost.  A connection whose destination cannot be determined exactly is
    refused with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED, and the message says why.

    Connections whose values were chosen by code that no such sandbox governs are made as before.

    @section odbc_sandbox_drivers Drivers

    The driver is the \c DRIVER of the connection string or of its DSN: a driver name from \c odbcinst.ini or the
    path of its library, which the driver is recognized by.

    |!Driver|!Destination|!Connection to the checked address
    |PostgreSQL (psqlODBC)|\c Servername or \c Server, \c Port (default 5432)|<tt>pqopt={hostaddr=...}</tt>; the host name is kept for TLS
    |MySQL and MariaDB (Connector/ODBC)|\c Server, \c Port (default 3306)|the server is replaced by the address; a connection that verifies the server's host name (\c SSLMODE=VERIFY_IDENTITY, \c SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT) is refused

    @section odbc_sandbox_refused Connections that are Refused

    - connections with the Microsoft ODBC Driver for SQL Server and the FreeTDS driver: the server can route the
      connection to another server during the login (TDS routing), which the driver follows without a check
    - connections with any other driver (named in the message), including the SQLite driver
    - a DSN that is not defined, a connection string with both \c DSN and \c DRIVER, and file DSNs
    - local sockets and empty hosts, and lists of servers
    - libpq options set by the connection itself (\c pqopt)

    @since odbc 2.0
*/
